{:edb/db-before-t 1944 :edb/db-after-t 1945 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535257 50 #inst "2026-10-08T20:34:40.779Z" 13194139535257 true] [17592186091967 1100 "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186091967 1100 "What is the first term that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n$\\dfrac 1 {32},\\: \\dfrac 1 4,\\: 2,\\: 16,\\: \\ldots$" 13194139535257 false] [17592186091967 1145 "The first term of the sequence is\n\n$$\na_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}\n$$\n\nand the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{32} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{64} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{8^{2}} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8^{-2} \\cdot 8^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 8^{n - 3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 8^{n - 3} & > 2000000 \\\\ 8^{n - 3} & > 1000000 \\\\ \\log (8^{n - 3}) & > \\log (1000000) \\\\ (n - 3)\\log (8) & > \\log (1000000) \\\\ n - 3 & > (\\log (1000000))/(\\log (8)) \\\\ n & > (\\log (1000000))/(\\log (8)) + 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000$." 13194139535257 false] [17592186091967 1145 "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=\\dfrac{1}{32}$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {4}\\right)} {\\left(\\dfrac 1 {32} \\right)} = 8.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{64}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{-2}\\cdot {8}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {8}^{n-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,000\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{n-3} & >2\\,000\\,000 \\\\ {8}^{n-3} & >1\\,000\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({8}^{n-3}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(1\\,000\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(8)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (1\\,000\\, 000)} {\\log (8)} + 3 \\approx 9.643.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,000\\,000.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186091968 1100 "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots\n$$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186091968 1100 "What is the first term that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$ in the following geometric sequence?\n$16,\\: 8,\\: 4,\\: 2,\\: \\ldots$" 13194139535257 false] [17592186091968 1145 "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16$, and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16} = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 16 \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2^{4} \\cdot 2^{1 - n} \\\\\n&= 2^{5 - n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{5 - n} & < 0.0000001 \\\\ \\log (2^{5 - n}) & < \\log (0.0000001) \\\\ 5 - n & < (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2)) \\\\ -n & < (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2)) - 5 \\\\ n & > 5 - (\\log (0.0000001))/(\\log (2))\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac {\\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1$." 13194139535257 false] [17592186091968 1145 "The first term of the sequence is $a_1=16,$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac 8 {16}  = \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =16\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{5-n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,000\\,1.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,000\\,1 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,000\\,1)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n5 - \\dfrac { \\log (0.000\\, 000\\, 1)} {\\log (2)} \\approx 28.253.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $29$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0. 000\\,000\\,1.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186091969 1100 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54$, and the common ratio is $3$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000$?" 13194139535257 false] [17592186091969 1100 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $54,$ and the common ratio is $3.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,000?$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186091969 1145 "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and  $r= 3,$ we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1}\\[3pt]\n\t\ta_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1}\\[3pt]\n\t54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3\\[3pt]\n\t54&=27a_1\\[3pt]\n\ta_1&=2\n\t}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000.$ So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >1\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(3)^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186091969 1145 "Since we are given that $a_{4}= 54$ and $r= 3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for $a_1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} a_n&=a_1\\cdot r^{n-1} \\\\[3pt] a_4&=a_1\\cdot 3^{4-1} \\\\[3pt] 54&=a_1\\cdot 3^3 \\\\[3pt] 54&=27a_1 \\\\[3pt] a_1&=2 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,000$. So, we set up and solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 3^{n - 1} & > 1000 \\\\ 3^{n - 1} & > 500 \\\\ \\log (3)^{n - 1} & > \\log (500) \\\\ (n - 1)\\log (3) & > \\log (500) \\\\ n - 1 & > (\\log (500))/(\\log (3)) \\\\ n & > (\\log (500))/(\\log (3)) + 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\log (500)} {\\log (3)} + 1 \\approx 6.657\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,000$." 13194139535257 false] [17592186092307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340145 13194139535257 true] [17592186092307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340148 13194139535257 true] [17592186092307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340152 13194139535257 true] [17592186092307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340155 13194139535257 true] [17592186092307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340189 13194139535257 true] [17592186092308 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340138 13194139535257 true] [17592186092308 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340159 13194139535257 true] [17592186092308 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340162 13194139535257 true] [17592186092308 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340175 13194139535257 true] [17592186092309 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340165 13194139535257 true] [17592186092309 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340168 13194139535257 true] [17592186092309 1020 #edb/ref 17592186340182 13194139535257 true] [17592186092640 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535257 true] [17592186092640 1100 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n\n$2020$?" 13194139535257 false] [17592186092640 1100 "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $5$, and the $6$th term is $40.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$2\\,020?$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186092640 1145 "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=5$ and ${a}_{6}=40$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{6-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=5$ and  $r=2$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{3-1} \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =4{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{n-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $2\\,020.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {2}^{n-3} & >2\\,020 \\\\ {2}^{n-3} & >404 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{2}^{n-3} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(404) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(404)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}+3≈11.658\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2\\,020.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186092744 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535257 true] [17592186092744 1100 "What is the first term that is less than $0.0006$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$64, 32, 16, 8, …$" 13194139535257 false] [17592186092744 1100 "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,6$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n64,\\,32,\\,16,\\,8,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186092744 1145 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=64$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{32}{64}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={2}^{6}\\cdot {2}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={2}^{7-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,6.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ {2}^{7-n} & <0.000\\,6 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(2)}^{7-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ (7-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(2) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6) \\\\ 7-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}-7 \\\\ n & >7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n7-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,6)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}≈17.703\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $18$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,6.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186092745 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535257 true] [17592186092745 1100 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $108$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}$. What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n\n$1984$?" 13194139535257 false] [17592186092745 1100 "The $4$th term of a geometric sequence is $108$, and the common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$1\\,984?$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186092745 1145 "Since we are given that ${a}_{4}=108$ and  $r=\\frac{3}{2}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{4-1} \\\\ 108 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3} \\\\ 108 & =\\frac{27}{8}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $1\\,984.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >1\\,984 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >62 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(62) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(62)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈11.179\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $1\\,984.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340138 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340139 13194139535257 true] [17592186340138 1089 "q-218498" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535257 true] [17592186340138 1100 "What is the first term that is less than $0.000\\,08$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n256,\\,64,\\,16,\\,4,\\,…\n$$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340138 1113 #uuid "8e846278-0be5-5403-b98f-fa7b7f911856" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340138 1145 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=256$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{64}{256}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =256\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={4}^{4}\\cdot {4}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={4}^{5-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,08.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ {4}^{5-n} & <0.000\\,08 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{5-n}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ (5-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08) \\\\ 5-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}-5 \\\\ n & >5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n5-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,08)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}≈11.805\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $12$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,08.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340140 13194139535257 true] [17592186340139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340141 13194139535257 true] [17592186340139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340142 1