8e7ea1" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340145 1145 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $2000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >2000 \\\\ 10\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >2000 \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1} & >200 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({(\\frac{3}{2})}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(200) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(200) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(200)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}+1≈14.067.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $15$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $2000.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340147 13194139535257 true] [17592186340146 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535257 true] [17592186340146 1041 "field-1" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340146 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340147 13194139535257 true] [17592186340146 1110 #uuid "07a83989-7eb7-504c-9634-dd2b08c1d7bb" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340147 1034 "15" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340147 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535257 true] [17592186340147 1111 #uuid "c32e71dd-28eb-54d3-97e4-9abdefb87f01" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340148 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340149 13194139535257 true] [17592186340148 1089 "q-277931" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340148 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535257 true] [17592186340148 1100 "What is the first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n2,\\,8,\\,32,\\,128,\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that exceeds $58\\,000$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139535257 true] [17592186340148 1113 #uuid "5f764e4f-dcbe-509f-94dd-fe5956c0700e" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340148 1145 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{8}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be greater than $58\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & >58\\,000 \\\\ 2\\cdot {4}^{n-1} & >58\\,000 \\\\ {4}^{n-1} & >29\\,000 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({4}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(29\\,000)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)}+1≈8.412.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $9$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $58\\,000.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340150 13194139535257 true] [17592186340149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340151 13194139535257 true] [17592186340149 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535257 true] [17592186340149 1041 "field-1" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340149 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340150 13194139535257 true] [17592186340149 1110 #uuid "aa22889a-e180-5804-af5a-616418acce8e" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340150 1034 "9" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535257 true] [17592186340150 1111 #uuid "938310c5-7318-51ff-b48f-81d22cf4f0f8" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340151 1034 "8" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535257 true] [17592186340151 1111 #uuid "9a3c0c07-f3e7-5ad2-a892-aee17b377bc6" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340152 1017 #edb/r