g a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n4-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,31)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(5)}≈9.020.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,31.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340160 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340161 13194139535257 true] [17592186340160 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535257 true] [17592186340160 1041 "field-1" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340160 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340161 13194139535257 true] [17592186340160 1110 #uuid "2b06e354-b06c-5291-93e2-1eb43a0ddc9b" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340161 1034 "10" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535257 true] [17592186340161 1111 #uuid "e15ea815-80fe-5f41-b8f0-cf6d04177ce9" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340162 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340163 13194139535257 true] [17592186340162 1089 "q-278053" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340162 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535257 true] [17592186340162 1100 "What is the first term that is less than $0.004$ in the following geometric sequence?\n\n$$\n20,\\,4,\\,\\frac{4}{5},\\,\\frac{4}{25},\\,…\n$$\n\nThe first term that is less than $0.004$ is the\n{{field-1}}th term." 13194139535257 true] [17592186340162 1113 #uuid "5ef379ee-1cf9-5420-a7f1-35b3b4d831c7" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340162 1145 "The first term of the sequence is ${a}_{1}=20$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the sequence can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.004.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & <0.004 \\\\ 20{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <0.004 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\frac{0.004}{20} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{n-1} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we reversed the inequality sign when divided by $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ because $\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})$ is a negative number. \n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,2)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})}+1≈6.292.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $7$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.004.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340164 13194139535257 true] [17592186340163 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535257 true] [17592186340163 1041 "field-1" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340163 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340164 13194139535257 true] [17592186340163 1110 #uuid "69a459ad-4afb-5faa-afe3-4007a1a84362" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340164 1034 "7" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535257 true] [17592186340164 1111 #uuid "1e91597f-d0b4-547b-a358-e75e3ee2ab64" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340165 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340166 13194139535257 true] [17592186340165 1089 "q-278078" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340165 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535257 true] [17592186340165 1100 "The $6$th term of a geometric sequence is $972$, and the common ratio is $3.$ The first term of this sequence to exceed $30\\,000$ is the{{field-1}}th term." 13194139535257 true] [17592186340165 1113 #uuid "1cdf5899-02fb-5458-b322-2c659223d357" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340165 1145 "Since we are given that ${a}_{6}=972$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 972 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 972 & =243{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{972}{243} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {3}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $30\\,000.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {3}^{n-1} & >30\\,000 \\\\ {3}^{n-1} & >7\\,500 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{n-1}) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ (n-1)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500) \\\\ n-1 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(7\\,500)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+1≈9.122.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $10$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $30\\,000.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340167 13194139535257 true] [17592186340166 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535257 true] [17592186340166 1041 "field-1" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340166 1104 #edb/ref 17592186340167 13194139535257 true] [17592186340166 1110 #uuid "f1250a89-01c6-58e3-8390-be66cc05a9e2" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340167 1034 "10" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535257 true] [17592186340167 1111 #uuid "f92173f0-767e-5928-9d9d-c25e82721ff3" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340168 1017 #edb/ref 17592186340169 13194139535257 true] [17592186340168 1089 "q-39656" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340168 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535257 true] [17592186340168 1100 "The $2$nd term of a geometric sequence with a positive common ratio is $\\frac{3}{2}$, and the $4$th term is $\\frac{2}{3}.$ Given that all terms of the sequence are positive, what is the first term of this sequence to be less than\n$0.000\\,11?$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340168 1113 #uuid "71e1875b-fe8a-53a0-ac17-ab2cc533cc26" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340168 1145 "Since we are given that ${a}_{2}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{\\frac{2}{3}}{\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ r & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the common ratio is positive, we take the positive value. Now, since ${a}_{4}=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  $r=\\frac{2}{3}$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} \\\\ {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1-n} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want the $n$th term to be less than $0.000\\,11.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <0.000\\,11 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{3-n} & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ (3-n)\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) & <\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11) \\\\ 3-n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\ -n & <\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}-3 \\\\ n & >3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n3-\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(0.000\\,11)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})}≈25.480\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $26$th term is the first term of the sequence that is less than $0.000\\,11.$" 13194139535257 true] [17592186340169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340170 13194139535257 true] [17592186340169 1000 #edb/ref 17592186340171 13194139535257 true] [1